00:16:51.280 --> 00:16:55.193
their substance and more riotous living.

00:16:56.530 --> 00:17:01.310
So while Marx agrees with
the logical necessity

00:17:01.310 --> 00:17:04.830
of postulating, a previous
accumulation of stocks

00:17:04.830 --> 00:17:08.560
as a the point of
departure for capitalism,

00:17:08.560 --> 00:17:12.220
he also challenges the
idyllic presentation

00:17:12.220 --> 00:17:15.470
of the concrete historical
process that enabled

00:17:15.470 --> 00:17:18.050
the accumulation of these period stocks.

00:17:18.050 --> 00:17:22.940
Moreover, and this is
quite a crucial point

00:17:22.940 --> 00:17:27.420
Marx also intends to show
that again, the accumulation

00:17:27.420 --> 00:17:30.780
of previous stocks is
a necessary condition,

00:17:30.780 --> 00:17:32.950
but not a sufficient one

00:17:32.950 --> 00:17:35.977
for explaining the historical
emergency of capitalism.

00:17:35.977 --> 00:17:38.910
And so something else, must be part

00:17:38.910 --> 00:17:39.973
of the equation.

00:17:41.700 --> 00:17:46.340
plunder, conquest, robbery,
and murder have characterized

00:17:46.340 --> 00:17:48.650
most of human history at
least when we speak about

00:17:48.650 --> 00:17:50.920
class societies in particular

00:17:50.920 --> 00:17:53.180
in this direct forms of domination

00:17:53.180 --> 00:17:57.500
and violence can not alone
explain what is distinctive

00:17:57.500 --> 00:18:00.300
about capitalism with regard
to other modes of production.

00:18:00.300 --> 00:18:03.200
So, in other words Marx is here looking

00:18:03.200 --> 00:18:07.383
for the missing element in the explanation

00:18:09.290 --> 00:18:11.430
of the historical emergence of capitalism.

00:18:11.430 --> 00:18:14.300
And this missing element
has to do with that deep

00:18:14.300 --> 00:18:16.803
profound transformation
in class relations.

00:18:20.918 --> 00:18:22.950
Let's see the next slide.

00:18:22.950 --> 00:18:24.020
No, Oh God.

00:18:24.020 --> 00:18:24.853
Yes, here.

00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:27.793
This is the slide I was looking for.

00:18:28.920 --> 00:18:33.730
So as he writes "in themselves

00:18:33.730 --> 00:18:36.620
money and commodities are no more capital

00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:40.620
than the means of production
and of subsistence are.

00:18:40.620 --> 00:18:43.670
They need to be transformed into capital

00:18:43.670 --> 00:18:46.780
but this transformation
can itself only take place

00:18:46.780 --> 00:18:50.430
under particular circumstances,
which meet together

00:18:50.430 --> 00:18:51.880
at this point.

00:18:51.880 --> 00:18:53.830
The confrontation of and contact

00:18:53.830 --> 00:18:57.580
between two very different
kinds of commodity owners.

00:18:57.580 --> 00:18:59.580
On the one hand, the owners

00:18:59.580 --> 00:19:03.047
of money means of production
means of subsistence

00:19:03.047 --> 00:19:05.560
who are eager to valorize
the sum of values

00:19:05.560 --> 00:19:09.180
they have appropriated by buying
the labor-power of others.

00:19:09.180 --> 00:19:12.200
On the other hand, free workers

00:19:12.200 --> 00:19:15.300
the sellers of their own labor-power

00:19:15.300 --> 00:19:17.413
and therefore the sellers of labor.

00:19:20.371 --> 00:19:21.750
(faintly speaking)

00:19:21.750 --> 00:19:22.820
Okay.

00:19:22.820 --> 00:19:27.820
So with this two concepts in mind.

00:19:29.710 --> 00:19:33.550
So in other words, the
fact that Marx wants to

00:19:35.080 --> 00:19:36.680
on the one hand challenge

00:19:36.680 --> 00:19:41.150
the idyllic myth of the
original capitalism.

00:19:41.150 --> 00:19:42.790
On the other hand

00:19:42.790 --> 00:19:47.220
he wants also to show what is
distinctive about capitalism.

00:19:47.220 --> 00:19:49.220
So what is the allows the transformation

00:19:49.220 --> 00:19:53.000
of money and commodities into capital.

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:57.720
I'm going to give a very brief summary

00:19:57.720 --> 00:20:01.153
of the main points of a Marxist arguments.

00:20:02.630 --> 00:20:06.110
And I would like to start
this by insisting on the fact

00:20:06.110 --> 00:20:11.110
that Marx is here talking
about what do you call it?

00:20:11.990 --> 00:20:14.633
The classical example of
primitive accumulation

00:20:14.633 --> 00:20:18.530
that is how primitive
accumulation concretely

00:20:18.530 --> 00:20:23.033
took place in England,
to an extent some many

00:20:24.640 --> 00:20:26.540
of these processes can be also extended

00:20:26.540 --> 00:20:28.900
to Western Europe and so on.

00:20:28.900 --> 00:20:31.190
But again, then we come back to this point

00:20:31.190 --> 00:20:33.800
the Marx is quite careful about saying

00:20:33.800 --> 00:20:37.300
that what is presenting
is not a universal history

00:20:37.300 --> 00:20:39.020
of the emergence of capitalism.

00:20:39.020 --> 00:20:43.773
It is the English history
of the emergence of capital.

00:20:44.833 --> 00:20:47.950
And we may wonder why he calls this

00:20:47.950 --> 00:20:50.630
the classical example I
think it may have to do

00:20:50.630 --> 00:20:53.290
with the fact that in the English example

00:20:53.290 --> 00:20:57.720
all the factors that can
contribute to a process

00:20:57.720 --> 00:21:00.393
of primitive accumulation
basically call us together.

00:21:01.660 --> 00:21:03.170
And we go back to this.

00:21:03.170 --> 00:21:08.170
So first point in Marx's
argument, as I already mentioned

00:21:12.550 --> 00:21:15.870
the existence of previous
stocks does not explain

00:21:15.870 --> 00:21:18.350
on its own how capitalism emerged.

00:21:18.350 --> 00:21:23.180
So Smith is not right in his account.

00:21:23.180 --> 00:21:27.273
What is needed is also
historical process conducive

00:21:28.360 --> 00:21:31.400
to the existence of a large mass

00:21:31.400 --> 00:21:36.400
of what Marx calls free
labor free in a edible sense

00:21:38.860 --> 00:21:43.490
free from a means of
production that is dispossessed

00:21:43.490 --> 00:21:46.920
and free to sell her labor power

00:21:46.920 --> 00:21:49.790
on the commodified on the labor market.

00:21:49.790 --> 00:21:52.582
We will go back to this
point in the second half

00:21:52.582 --> 00:21:56.970
of the lecture now in Marx's account

00:21:56.970 --> 00:21:58.430
this mass of free laborers

00:21:58.430 --> 00:22:01.710
in England was produced by centuries

00:22:02.701 --> 00:22:05.890
long process of separation of human beings

00:22:05.890 --> 00:22:08.450
from the conditions of
their own reproduction.

00:22:08.450 --> 00:22:10.370
That is from land,

00:22:10.370 --> 00:22:14.540
land that understood and
I follow here, for example

00:22:14.540 --> 00:22:17.980
Robert Nichols understood not as basically

00:22:17.980 --> 00:22:19.840
the ability to engage

00:22:19.840 --> 00:22:22.970
in what Marx would call
the metabolic exchange

00:22:22.970 --> 00:22:25.040
we measure for the sake

00:22:25.040 --> 00:22:27.550
of (mumbles) production of social life.

00:22:27.550 --> 00:22:32.550
So the separation from land,
it basically means the breaking

00:22:33.600 --> 00:22:37.950
of the metabolic exchange
with nature and the separation

00:22:37.950 --> 00:22:42.020
of the masses of people
from a direct access

00:22:42.020 --> 00:22:47.020
to what is needed for the
reproduction of social life.

00:22:50.911 --> 00:22:55.440
Second key point in Marx's argument

00:22:55.440 --> 00:22:57.960
is historical process of
separation in England?

00:22:57.960 --> 00:23:01.340
There was anything but peaceful.

00:23:01.340 --> 00:23:03.450
It was not an ideal.

00:23:03.450 --> 00:23:07.160
In fact, as he writes it was written

00:23:07.160 --> 00:23:10.873
in the annals of mankind in
letters of blood and fire.

00:23:12.560 --> 00:23:13.870
So in the chapters

00:23:13.870 --> 00:23:16.700
on the expropriation of
agricultural population

00:23:16.700 --> 00:23:18.950
and in the chapters on
the bloody legislation

00:23:20.050 --> 00:23:22.890
against the expropriated Marx documents

00:23:22.890 --> 00:23:25.030
the various processes that have led

00:23:25.030 --> 00:23:30.030
to this outcome between let's
say, 15th and 17 18th century.

00:23:32.720 --> 00:23:37.720
So these processes include
the foreseeable expulsion

00:23:38.060 --> 00:23:43.060
of persons from the
land by feudal landlords

00:23:44.070 --> 00:23:47.670
by means of extra economic
and state violence.

00:23:47.670 --> 00:23:50.853
And they abolition of a few protections,

00:23:51.730 --> 00:23:53.610
it's worth not even noticing here

00:23:53.610 --> 00:23:57.670
that Marx here is not really emphasizing

00:24:00.350 --> 00:24:04.250
let's say that evolutionary
aspect of this process

00:24:04.250 --> 00:24:06.960
in terms of in progressive terms

00:24:06.960 --> 00:24:10.280
on the contrary to what we may find

00:24:10.280 --> 00:24:14.437
for example, in the 1848 manifesto

00:24:14.437 --> 00:24:16.890
this history is really
presented as a tragedy.

00:24:16.890 --> 00:24:19.300
So in other words (mumbles)

00:24:19.300 --> 00:24:21.087
was an oppressive model production.

00:24:21.087 --> 00:24:24.390
And so on it also granted some protections

00:24:24.390 --> 00:24:29.390
to peasants access to come
to the commons granted use

00:24:30.460 --> 00:24:33.183
in continuous use of land and so on.

00:24:34.648 --> 00:24:39.648
So the first process in
this separation is again,

00:24:43.620 --> 00:24:45.880
the violent forcible expulsion

00:24:45.880 --> 00:24:49.720
from land and abolition
of feudal protections

00:24:50.570 --> 00:24:53.160
and the consequent
progressive depopulation

00:24:53.160 --> 00:24:56.390
of the countryside and
increase in your organization.

00:24:56.390 --> 00:25:01.217
So large movements of
population towards cities.

00:25:03.420 --> 00:25:04.730
The enclosure of common lands

00:25:04.730 --> 00:25:07.530
to which peasants used to have access

00:25:07.530 --> 00:25:09.963
for pasturage would collect it and so on.

00:25:11.377 --> 00:25:16.160
And then a series of
bloody state legislations

00:25:16.160 --> 00:25:18.870
meant to discipline the expropriated

00:25:18.870 --> 00:25:21.730
and to force them to
sell their labor power

00:25:21.730 --> 00:25:25.463
under the threat of enslavement or death.

00:25:26.468 --> 00:25:28.140
I want to insist on this point,

00:25:28.140 --> 00:25:31.070
because although as we will see

00:25:31.070 --> 00:25:35.690
Marx will also say that once

00:25:35.690 --> 00:25:38.570
in place capital is we
create the working class

00:25:38.570 --> 00:25:41.350
that will naturalized
capitalist social relations

00:25:41.350 --> 00:25:43.790
and be forced to work

00:25:43.790 --> 00:25:47.020
by the silent economic compulsion.

00:25:47.020 --> 00:25:48.170
We also need to take

00:25:48.170 --> 00:25:52.660
into account disorder
element that has to do

00:25:52.660 --> 00:25:55.430
with the fact that
there is nothing natural

00:25:55.430 --> 00:25:59.850
in selling out once on
labor power, as a commodity

00:25:59.850 --> 00:26:02.560
and people had to be forced to do this

00:26:02.560 --> 00:26:04.870
and had to be forced to do this

00:26:04.870 --> 00:26:09.870
by a display of an immobilization
of enormous violence.

00:26:10.940 --> 00:26:12.970
And we'd go back to this point

00:26:12.970 --> 00:26:17.370
because as I will argue later in fact

00:26:17.370 --> 00:26:21.320
this disciplining of
labor also takes place

00:26:21.320 --> 00:26:23.913
within capitalist accumulation proper.

00:26:24.900 --> 00:26:26.990
So it is not really the case

00:26:26.990 --> 00:26:29.340
that the silent economic compulsion

00:26:29.340 --> 00:26:32.443
does all the work, but
we will go back to this.

00:26:34.290 --> 00:26:38.190
Then the other elements
that Marx emphasizes

00:26:38.190 --> 00:26:39.670
in this process are the emergence

00:26:39.670 --> 00:26:42.000
of a class of rich farmers.

00:26:42.000 --> 00:26:44.240
Those who survived process of expulsion

00:26:44.240 --> 00:26:46.140
from the land, from which the class

00:26:46.140 --> 00:26:51.040
of industrial capitalist will
arise, the growing importance

00:26:51.040 --> 00:26:56.040
of public debt or credit in
of the modern fiscal system

00:26:56.640 --> 00:26:59.790
which will abolish the
independence of the state

00:26:59.790 --> 00:27:03.110
from capital and therefore
create the material conditions

00:27:03.110 --> 00:27:05.833
for the state to become
the guardian of capital.

00:27:13.470 --> 00:27:16.890
Quite importantly, Marx
also demystifies the story

00:27:16.890 --> 00:27:19.140
of the industrious accumulation

00:27:19.140 --> 00:27:23.360
of previous stocks or capital
that we find for example

00:27:23.360 --> 00:27:26.220
is myth by stressing the role played

00:27:26.220 --> 00:27:29.340
by colonization and
therefore by plunder robbery

00:27:29.340 --> 00:27:34.340
and slave meant a merger in
this idyllic accumulation.

00:27:34.612 --> 00:27:37.370
So I will share my screen again

00:27:37.370 --> 00:27:40.823
so that we can have a look
at two passages about this.

00:27:44.270 --> 00:27:46.307
So first passage,

00:27:46.307 --> 00:27:48.870
"The discovery of gold
and silver in America

00:27:48.870 --> 00:27:53.148
the extirpation enslavement,
and entombment in mines

00:27:53.148 --> 00:27:56.430
of the indigenous
population of the continent

00:27:56.430 --> 00:27:58.660
the beginnings of the conquest and plunder

00:27:58.660 --> 00:28:00.900
of India and the conversion of Africa

00:28:00.900 --> 00:28:03.010
into a present for the commercial hunting

00:28:03.010 --> 00:28:06.950
of black skins and all things
which characterize the dawn

00:28:06.950 --> 00:28:09.660
of the era of capitalist production.

00:28:09.660 --> 00:28:12.460
These idyllic proceedings
are the chief moments

00:28:12.460 --> 00:28:14.793
of primitive accumulation."

00:28:16.170 --> 00:28:17.443
Then a second quote,

00:28:22.797 --> 00:28:26.220
"The treatment of the indigenous
population was of course

00:28:26.220 --> 00:28:31.220
most frightful in plantation-colonies
set up exclusively

00:28:31.540 --> 00:28:35.650
for the export trade, such
as the West Indies, any rich

00:28:35.650 --> 00:28:39.340
and well-populated countries,
such as Mexico and India

00:28:39.340 --> 00:28:41.570
they were given over to plunder.

00:28:41.570 --> 00:28:44.380
But even in the colonies
properly so-called

00:28:44.380 --> 00:28:45.780
the Christian character

00:28:45.780 --> 00:28:48.207
of primitive accumulation
did not belie itself."

00:28:49.590 --> 00:28:52.980
So colonization played a crucial
role in two different ways

00:28:52.980 --> 00:28:54.690
at least in this account.

00:28:54.690 --> 00:28:58.760
First, the violent transfer
of immense resources

00:28:58.760 --> 00:29:02.859
from the non non European war to Europe

00:29:02.859 --> 00:29:04.100
either through plunder

00:29:04.100 --> 00:29:06.820
or through enslavement
in plantation colonies

00:29:06.820 --> 00:29:10.927
where forced labor produced
the raw materials necessary

00:29:10.927 --> 00:29:14.183
for the development later on
industrial capital in Europe.

00:29:15.370 --> 00:29:19.100
And colonization also provided a market

00:29:19.100 --> 00:29:21.460
for the European emerging manufacturers.

00:29:21.460 --> 00:29:25.597
So this is another quote.

00:29:25.597 --> 00:29:27.330
"The colonies provided a market

00:29:27.330 --> 00:29:29.720
for the budding manufacturers

00:29:29.720 --> 00:29:31.760
and a vast increase in accumulation

00:29:31.760 --> 00:29:33.890
which was guaranteed
by the mother country's

00:29:33.890 --> 00:29:37.000
monopoly of the market,
the treasures captured

00:29:37.000 --> 00:29:40.930
outside Europe by undisguised
looting, enslavement

00:29:40.930 --> 00:29:43.850
and murder flowed back
to the mother country

00:29:43.850 --> 00:29:46.257
and were turned into capital there."

00:29:49.960 --> 00:29:54.313
Okay, so finally,

00:29:55.450 --> 00:29:59.630
one of the aspects highlighted
by Marx as a consequence

00:29:59.630 --> 00:30:03.355
of expropriation in separation
from the land is the end

00:30:03.355 --> 00:30:07.500
of self subsistence
oriented peasant economy

00:30:07.500 --> 00:30:12.500
at least as a generalized way
of reproducing social life.

00:30:14.330 --> 00:30:18.380
Now, while formerly the
person family produce means

00:30:18.380 --> 00:30:20.120
of subsistence and raw materials

00:30:20.120 --> 00:30:22.760
mostly for their own consumption

00:30:22.760 --> 00:30:27.760
the large scale farmer produces
directly for the market.

00:30:28.130 --> 00:30:32.970
Now, Marx does not addressing
capital the consequences

00:30:32.970 --> 00:30:35.220
of this process from the
viewpoint of the transformation

00:30:35.220 --> 00:30:38.610
of the family and from the viewpoint

00:30:38.610 --> 00:30:43.610
of the transformational women's
oppression under capitalism.

00:30:44.100 --> 00:30:46.760
But this is an argument
that will be developed

00:30:46.760 --> 00:30:49.520
by Marxist feminist and
socialist feminist orders

00:30:49.520 --> 00:30:51.540
in especially in the 20th century

00:30:53.270 --> 00:30:56.260
in so far, as much as
feminists will be the ones

00:30:56.260 --> 00:30:57.093
who will fully

00:30:57.093 --> 00:31:02.060
grasp the significance
of this transformation

00:31:03.490 --> 00:31:06.100
and this tragedy

00:31:06.100 --> 00:31:09.140
of this transformation in
providing an explanation

00:31:09.140 --> 00:31:11.450
for capitalist forms of gender oppression.

00:31:11.450 --> 00:31:14.230
So in other words, and I know
you have already listened

00:31:14.230 --> 00:31:16.550
to Tithi Bhattacharya
on social reproduction

00:31:16.550 --> 00:31:20.117
and so on, in other
words, one of the effects

00:31:20.117 --> 00:31:22.810
of primitive accumulation
is the separation

00:31:22.810 --> 00:31:25.750
between a production social reproduction

00:31:25.750 --> 00:31:29.350
the subordination of social
production to production

00:31:29.350 --> 00:31:32.420
which constitutes the material framework.

00:31:32.420 --> 00:31:33.700
The social material framework

00:31:33.700 --> 00:31:36.300
for continued gender oppression

00:31:36.300 --> 00:31:39.710
in specifically is for
myself from this viewpoint

00:31:39.710 --> 00:31:41.460
gender oppression is not a remnant

00:31:41.460 --> 00:31:43.093
of a pre-capitalist past.

00:31:44.240 --> 00:31:46.840
It actually, it continues under capitalism

00:31:46.840 --> 00:31:50.453
but in new forms and
through new mechanisms.

00:31:51.550 --> 00:31:55.760
So in a sense necessary dated or somebody

00:31:55.760 --> 00:31:58.110
very logical capitalist
accumulation, at least

00:31:58.110 --> 00:32:00.623
in terms of necessarily consequences,

00:32:01.741 --> 00:32:06.360
Maria (mumbles) have
also famously emphasized

00:32:07.360 --> 00:32:08.760
the direct discipline

00:32:08.760 --> 00:32:11.360
in violence exercised on the bodies

00:32:11.360 --> 00:32:13.787
of women within a primitive accumulation.

00:32:14.721 --> 00:32:17.160
Maria Mies in particular insisted

00:32:17.160 --> 00:32:18.690
on the impact of colonization

00:32:19.620 --> 00:32:22.143
on women and women's conditions of life.

00:32:23.150 --> 00:32:26.640
Silvia Federici focusing more

00:32:26.640 --> 00:32:28.863
on primitive accumulation in Europe,

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:33.700
basically in caliber

00:32:33.700 --> 00:32:38.700
which made the argument
that a specific process

00:32:39.070 --> 00:32:42.740
of disciplining of women's bodies was put

00:32:42.740 --> 00:32:47.740
in place in a specific process
of expropriation of women

00:32:48.520 --> 00:32:52.830
from both their spheres
of expertise and a means

00:32:52.830 --> 00:32:57.440
of subsistence that they did
have in person societies.

00:32:57.440 --> 00:33:02.290
Now, I do not really have the
time to go into these debates.

00:33:03.660 --> 00:33:06.990
And, but I would say just this

00:33:06.990 --> 00:33:09.710
that while I do have reservations

00:33:09.710 --> 00:33:13.130
on several details of the
picture articulated in particular

00:33:13.130 --> 00:33:16.460
by Silvia Federici, including the role

00:33:16.460 --> 00:33:19.210
of which hands in this process or the role

00:33:19.210 --> 00:33:24.210
that Federici attributes to
the body at the same time.

00:33:24.630 --> 00:33:28.480
I do think that her research, as well

00:33:28.480 --> 00:33:33.400
as Maria Mies have had the
merit of poking the attention

00:33:34.270 --> 00:33:36.960
to the way in which
primitive accumulation also

00:33:36.960 --> 00:33:40.200
implied a deep transformation
of gender relations

00:33:40.200 --> 00:33:43.770
and forms disciplines impacting on women.

00:33:43.770 --> 00:33:44.750
So in other words

00:33:44.750 --> 00:33:49.140
I do deeply appreciate
the theoretical move.

00:33:49.140 --> 00:33:51.660
I'm not so sure about
the historical argument

00:33:51.660 --> 00:33:56.660
and I think more historical
work needs to be done

